---

{% only_dark %}
![Appwrite Analytics dashboard](/images/docs/analytics/dark/cover.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Appwrite Analytics dashboard](/images/docs/analytics/cover.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Appwrite **Analytics** shows how people find and use your websites and apps. Add tracking with the Web or Flutter SDK, or send events over REST. Each property's dashboard then shows your visitors, where they came from, the pages they viewed, where they are, and the devices they use, next to the events you define, such as signups and purchases.

Analytics works without cookies or identifiers stored on the visitor's device, and labels traffic from search crawlers, AI crawlers, AI assistants, and other bots, so you can see how much of your traffic comes from people.

{% info title="Appwrite Cloud" %}
Analytics is available on Appwrite Cloud. Your organization plan sets how many events you can send each month and how far back you can see your data. See [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits).
{% /info %}

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

1. **Property**: A property is one website or app you track, with its own property ID, settings, and dashboard. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties).
2. **Events**: Your site or app sends an event for each pageview and action. The tracking helpers in the Web and Flutter SDKs send common events for you, and you add your own. See [Events](/docs/products/analytics/events).
3. **Enrichment**: For each event, Appwrite works out the page, the traffic source, the location, the browser and device, whether a bot sent it, and which visitor and visit it belongs to.
4. **Reports**: The dashboard turns events into metrics and breakdowns for any date range, with filters and comparisons. You can also export them, and read them from your code with the Analytics API.

